    "text": "The standard hardware-Trojan taxonomy (Karri, Rajendran, Rosenfeld, Tehranipoor) describes a Trojan by insertion phase, abstraction level, activation mechanism (trigger), effect (payload) and location. Triggers may exploit don't-care conditions, specific data patterns in particular memory addresses, or dedicated rare inputs; combinational triggers are drawn from primary inputs and internal nodes, while sequential triggers use state elements such as counters to fire after an input sequence or time period. Payloads: the most common are sensitive-information leakage and denial of service. Payloads are explicit (they alter internal signals/outputs) or implicit (the signals are used only as trigger stimulus). In cryptographic hardware, high-impact payloads include leaking the key or device secret, weakening the cipher (reducing effective entropy), and forging or bypassing authentication-tag verification so tampered ciphertext is accepted.",
    "metadata": {"type": "security-method", "topic": "hardware-trojan", "title": "HW Trojan taxonomy"}
  },
  {
    "id": "SRC-EEPROM-EXTRACT",
    "source": "Extracting firmware from EEPROM, flash and eMMC (thyrasec.com)",
    "url": "https://www.thyrasec.com/blog/extracting-firmware-from-eeprom-flash-and-emmc/",
    "text": "I2C EEPROMs commonly hold configuration and sometimes cryptographic keys. I2C is an unencrypted interface that is easy to sniff: with the board powered, a Bus Pirate or logic analyzer clipped onto the SDA/SCL lines passively captures all traffic, and EEPROM access typically happens at startup, so an attacker can capture whatever a host (MCU or FPGA) reads from the EEPROM at power-up without desoldering. In-circuit clips attach without soldering and the board stays powered so other bus masters keep operating. Running binwalk and checking entropy reveals whether captured data is encrypted; if it is, the key is stored elsewhere (e.g. secure internal memory). Writing/programming an EEPROM is more intrusive and involves handling the write-protect pin. Passive bus observation is low-risk; active readout or modification risks damaging the chip or board.",
    "metadata": {"type": "security-method", "topic": "eeprom-extraction", "title": "EEPROM/flash extraction"}
  }
]

