  "id": "SRC-CVSS-SPEC",
  "source": "FIRST CVSS v3.1 Specification Document",
  "url": "https://www.first.org/cvss/v3.1/specification-document",
  "text": "CVSS v3.1 Attack Vector Physical (AV:P) requires the attacker to physically touch or manipulate the vulnerable component (e.g., attaching hardware to a bus). Local (AV:L) applies when the attacker exploits the vulnerability through local access such as a console or command interface, or an attacker-controlled local process, without physically manipulating the component. A vulnerability reachable through a device's existing local command interface, rather than by attaching hardware, is therefore AV:L. Scope is Unchanged (S:U) when an exploited vulnerability can only affect resources managed by the same security authority. Scope is Changed (S:C) when it can affect resources beyond the security scope of the vulnerable component's security authority, i.e. it breaches a security or trust boundary and impacts components governed by a different authority. A security authority is a mechanism that defines and enforces access control.",
  "metadata": {"type": "standard", "topic": "cvss", "title": "CVSS v3.1 AV and Scope"}
},
{
  "id": "SRC-AEAD-KEYLEAK",
  "source": "AEAD security properties (NIST SP 800-232 Ascon; standard AEAD definitions)",
  "url": "https://laoutaris.cc/blog/ascon_guide/",
  "text": "The security of an AEAD scheme such as Ascon-AEAD128 rests entirely on the secrecy of the symmetric key. An attacker who obtains the key can decrypt any captured ciphertext (total loss of confidentiality) and can also compute a valid authentication tag for any chosen ciphertext, so the accelerator will accept attacker-forged messages as authentic (total loss of integrity). Thus a key disclosure is simultaneously a confidentiality and an integrity compromise. Separately, nonce (IV) reuse under a fixed key in a sponge AEAD like Ascon causes keystream reuse, which leaks relationships between plaintexts (a confidentiality break) and allows recovering one plaintext from another when one is known; nonce reuse alone does not by itself yield a universal tag forgery, because the capacity (secret) portion of the state is not directly exposed.",
  "metadata": {"type": "crypto-spec", "topic": "aead-key", "title": "AEAD key leakage and nonce reuse"}
},
